#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059416 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059779 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8946f811-40c4-5a19-a867-576c42364f15" :knowledge-point/title "Writing Biconditionals as Conditionals"}] :question/id #uuid "9df9c7d3-b06a-5f0c-be20-c0d24baa81ad" :question/math-academy-id "e-12715" :question/problem "Express the following statement as a conjunction of implications.\n\n$$\n\\neg P \\Leftrightarrow Q\n$$" :question/worked-solution "According to the definition of the biconditional, \n\n- $A \\Leftrightarrow B$ is true if $A\\Rightarrow B$ **and** $B\\Rightarrow A.$ Otherwise, it is false.\n\n- In other words, $A \\Leftrightarrow B$ is true if $A$ and $B$ have the same truth values. Otherwise, it is false.\n\nTherefore, in this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\neg P \\Leftrightarrow Q \\equiv (\\neg P \\Rightarrow Q) \\land (Q \\Rightarrow \\neg P).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059417 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059778 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "35e42262-e78c-5553-acfe-739684165622" :knowledge-point/title "Completing a Truth Table for an Equivalence"}] :question/id #uuid "b5abe855-4626-5575-a03e-04a377eef82b" :question/math-academy-id "e-12716" :question/problem "What are the missing entries in the following truth table?\n\n| $\\,P\\,$ | $\\,Q\\,$ | $\\, ( \\neg P \\land Q) \\Leftrightarrow Q \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\phantom{F}}$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\phantom{F}}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\phantom{F}}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\phantom{F}}$ |" :question/worked-solution "According to the definition of the biconditional, \n\n- $A \\Leftrightarrow B$ is true if $A\\Rightarrow B$ **and** $B\\Rightarrow A.$ Otherwise, it is false.\n\n- In other words, $A \\Leftrightarrow B$ is true if $A$ and $B$ have the same truth values. Otherwise, it is false.\n\nFirst, we add columns for $\\neg P$ and $\\neg P \\land Q{:}$\n\n| $\\,P\\,$ | $\\,Q\\,$ | $\\, \\neg P \\,$ | $\\, \\neg P \\land Q \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ |\n\nFinally, we add a column for $(\\neg P \\land Q) \\Leftrightarrow Q.$ \n\n| $\\,P\\,$ | $\\,Q\\,$ | $\\, \\neg P \\,$ | $\\, \\neg P \\land Q \\,$ | $\\, (\\neg P \\land Q) \\Leftrightarrow Q \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059418 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059777 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe8eb0bd-6d37-5eb8-98fd-796b2af992f7" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Equivalence Truth Table"}] :question/id #uuid "15d6b6c0-af70-5d22-bfdc-b2c0aaf98de5" :question/math-academy-id "e-12717" :question/problem "If $A$ is true and $B$ is true, what is the truth value of $A \\Leftrightarrow B ?$" :question/worked-solution "According to the definition of the biconditional, \n\n- $A \\Leftrightarrow B$ is true if $A\\Rightarrow B$ **and** $B\\Rightarrow A.$ Otherwise, it is false.\n\n- In other words, $A \\Leftrightarrow B$ is true if $A$ and $B$ have the same truth values. Otherwise, it is false.\n\nSo, when $A = \\textrm T$ and $B = \\textrm T,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A⇔B) & =(\\text{T}⇔\\text{T}) \\\\  & =\\text{T}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe complete truth table is shown below:\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,A \\Leftrightarrow B\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  |  |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059419 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059780 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e85e1e6-6a11-52e6-96d3-a768ced940d4" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Expressions Involving Equivalences"}] :question/id #uuid "51dc6bfc-d581-5972-9101-a80e614b33e4" :question/math-academy-id "e-12718" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are equivalent to $\\lnot (\\neg Y \\Leftrightarrow Z)?$\n\nI. $Y \\Rightarrow \\lnot Z$\n\nII. $(Y \\lor Z) \\Rightarrow (Y \\land Z)$\n\nIII. $(Y \\land Z) \\land (Y \\land \\lnot Z)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we use the definition of the biconditional statement to write the equivalence as a conjunction of implications: \n\n$$\n\\lnot (\\lnot Y \\Leftrightarrow Z) \n\\equiv \\lnot \\big[(\\lnot Y \\Rightarrow Z) \\land (Z \\Rightarrow \\lnot Y)\\big]\n$$\n\nNow, we represent all the implications using disjunctions and negations:\n\n$$\n\\lnot \\big[(\\lnot Y \\Rightarrow Z) \\land (Z \\Rightarrow \\lnot Y)\\big]\n\\equiv \\lnot \\big[(\\lnot \\lnot Y \\lor Z) \\land (\\lnot Z \\lor \\lnot Y)\\big]\n$$\n\nNext, we simplify the double negations:\n\n$$\n\\lnot \\big[(\\lnot \\lnot Y \\lor Z) \\land (\\lnot Z \\lor \\lnot Y)\\big] \n\\equiv \\lnot \\big[(Y \\lor Z) \\land (\\lnot Z \\lor \\lnot Y)\\big]\n$$\n\nNow, we use De Morgan's laws:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬[(Y∨Z)∧(¬Z∨¬Y)] & ≡¬(Y∨Z)∨¬(¬Z∨¬Y) \\\\  & ≡¬(Y∨Z)∨¬¬(Z∧Y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we again simplify the double negations and write down symbols in alphabetic order inside the parentheses using commutative laws:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(Y∨Z)∨¬¬(Z∧Y) & ≡¬(Y∨Z)∨(Z∧Y) \\\\  & ≡¬(Y∨Z)∨(Y∧Z) \\\\  & ≡(Y∨Z)⇒(Y∧Z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is only II."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411155 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059780 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e85e1e6-6a11-52e6-96d3-a768ced940d4" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Expressions Involving Equivalences"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "940f2589-9f73-5417-804b-2bb82c8143cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "¬B⇒A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411134} {:answer/id #uuid "b492421a-8fbc-5f3d-88d9-040402e7893f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(A∧B)∨¬(B∨A)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411138} {:answer/id #uuid "d3188347-6e03-5c7a-b90e-0a84321cc0f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(A∨B)∧¬(B∨A)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411140} {:answer/id #uuid "e4314ff2-9a8b-5692-b710-657e0db7b72c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(A∨B)∧¬(B∧A)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411141} {:answer/id #uuid "f72d28f7-58e9-5fb1-a702-3dc4acfad2ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(A∧B)∧¬(B∨A)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411145}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e4314ff2-9a8b-5692-b710-657e0db7b72c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(A∨B)∧¬(B∧A)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411141} :answer-field/id #uuid "e18107c3-8a03-5991-a93e-5223d254814d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411151}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "75186b51-1b0d-58e4-a5b9-b90965af20bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-107158" :question/problem "$¬A⇔B≡$" :question/worked-solution "First, we use the definition of the biconditional statement to write the equivalence as a conjunction of implications: \n\n$$\n¬A⇔B≡(¬A⇒B)∧(B⇒¬A)\n$$\n\nNow, we represent all the implications using disjunctions and negations:\n\n$$\n(¬A⇒B)∧(B⇒¬A)≡(¬¬A∨B)∧(¬B∨¬A)\n$$\n\nNext, we use De Morgan's laws:\n\n$$\n(¬¬A∨B)∧(¬B∨¬A)≡(¬¬A∨B)∧¬(B∧A)\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify the double negations:\n\n$$\n(¬¬A∨B)∧¬(B∧A)≡(A∨B)∧¬(B∧A)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059973 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059779 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8946f811-40c4-5a19-a867-576c42364f15" :knowledge-point/title "Writing Biconditionals as Conditionals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b3261c5-6787-508d-9460-d7cb1babe656" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "¬P⇒Q" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378370} {:answer/id #uuid "1d44f75c-a480-5eb9-84c6-69245777056e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "¬P⇒(P∨Q)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378371} {:answer/id #uuid "323ce835-3226-5d25-bae6-286340c7c8be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q⇒¬P" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378372} {:answer/id #uuid "6f643d78-b3e4-559a-a5a5-d8551f858df0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(P∨Q)⇒¬P" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378373} {:answer/id #uuid "56988074-e126-538b-b890-786b8b7d8574" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(P∨Q)⇔¬P" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378374}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6f643d78-b3e4-559a-a5a5-d8551f858df0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(P∨Q)⇒¬P" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378373} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e4195b3-bcb7-56ff-a316-e1df147b9d7a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378369}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fa3b578a-f7b2-5f35-99ec-63d7d2517930" :question/math-academy-id "q-107159" :question/problem "$$\n¬P⇔(P∨Q)\\,≡\\,[¬P⇒(P∨Q)]∧\n$$\n\nWhat expression should be inserted into the blank space above to give logically equivalent statements?" :question/worked-solution "According to the definition of the biconditional, \n\n- $A⇔B$ is true if $A⇒B$ **and** $B⇒A.$ Otherwise, it is false.\n\n- In other words, $A⇔B$ is true if $A$ and $B$ have the same truth values. Otherwise, it is false.\n\nTherefore, in this case, we have\n\n$$\n¬P⇔(P∨Q)\\,≡\\,[¬P⇒(P∨Q)]∧[\\,(P∨Q)⇒¬P\\,].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing part is $(P∨Q)⇒¬P.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378725 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059779 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8946f811-40c4-5a19-a867-576c42364f15" :knowledge-point/title "Writing Biconditionals as Conditionals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "98d5af90-8104-53d9-821b-af266f122c1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "S⇒R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378727} {:answer/id #uuid "33220c25-c7ee-5873-a263-b5585c309aa4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(R⇒S)∧(¬S⇒¬R)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378728} {:answer/id #uuid "13086fbd-18c6-5d09-aca3-5c649748ae8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(R⇒S)∨(S⇒R)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378729} {:answer/id #uuid "23e01a25-4374-57a1-972c-99e904067538" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(R⇒S)∧(S⇒R)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378730} {:answer/id #uuid "ac9afe32-4340-56b7-b718-6f9ca8577094" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R⇒S" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378731}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "23e01a25-4374-57a1-972c-99e904067538" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(R⇒S)∧(S⇒R)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378730} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4671e88-67ad-5276-9c5a-656604906e9f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378726}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "81939a3d-dab0-5753-b430-992f192c418c" :question/math-academy-id "q-1472" :question/problem "$R⇔S≡$" :question/worked-solution "According to the definition of the biconditional, \n\n- $A⇔B$ is true if $A⇒B$ **and** $B⇒A.$ Otherwise, it is false.\n\n- In other words, $A⇔B$ is true if $A$ and $B$ have the same truth values. Otherwise, it is false.\n\nSo in this case, we have\n\n$$\nR⇔S≡(R⇒S)∧(S⇒R).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411157 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059780 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e85e1e6-6a11-52e6-96d3-a768ced940d4" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Expressions Involving Equivalences"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0833f240-185e-532a-824f-4ccd738ff6d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(Y∧X)∨(¬X∧¬Y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411120} {:answer/id #uuid "81e51dfb-5df1-5588-964c-928acab1bcb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "X∧¬Y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411131} {:answer/id #uuid "8bda5940-fd50-546b-b1c8-6f4ae1ea16b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "¬X∧Y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411133} {:answer/id #uuid "999bcf3c-92e7-59d4-bcad-b3707c6d09dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(Y∧¬X)∧(X∧¬Y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411135} {:answer/id #uuid "9b327029-890c-51ee-9cd1-2d50c1493769" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(Y∧¬X)∨(X∧¬Y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411136}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b327029-890c-51ee-9cd1-2d50c1493769" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(Y∧¬X)∨(X∧¬Y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411136} :answer-field/id #uuid "67aed17a-d2cd-554a-99c0-e5486a53aa69" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411147}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "96aebafe-ff76-58ac-a04c-e47b82d4e2a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-182523" :question/problem "$¬(Y⇔X)≡$" :question/worked-solution "First, we use the definition of the biconditional statement to write the equivalence as a conjunction of implications: \n\n$$\n¬(Y⇔X)≡¬[(Y⇒X)∧(X⇒Y)]\n$$\n\nNow, we represent all the implications using disjunctions and negations:\n\n$$\n¬[(Y⇒X)∧(X⇒Y)]≡¬[(¬Y∨X)∧(¬X∨Y)]\n$$\n\nNext, we use De Morgan's laws:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬[(¬Y∨X)∧(¬X∨Y)] & ≡¬(¬Y∨X)∨¬(¬X∨Y) \\\\  & ≡(¬¬Y∧¬X)∨(¬¬X∧¬Y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify the double negations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(¬¬Y∧¬X)∨(¬¬X∧¬Y) & ≡(Y∧¬X)∨(X∧¬Y)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390017 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059777 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe8eb0bd-6d37-5eb8-98fd-796b2af992f7" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Equivalence Truth Table"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d2db2d8-b4eb-55d8-a90c-7ac7c8cc32a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "sometimes false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390019} {:answer/id #uuid "a4c7af80-2a03-5acb-8ed0-4bcb677f9c0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "sometimes true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390020} {:answer/id #uuid "30e90dd2-cd04-5639-85c0-102422a20adb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "always false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390021} {:answer/id #uuid "683f0f0e-96a5-5002-81bd-7349a286246c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "always true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390022}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "683f0f0e-96a5-5002-81bd-7349a286246c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "always true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390022} :answer-field/id #uuid "7176b171-88bc-5eb0-8c23-0863a35fb450" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390018}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9975dc7b-5885-54a3-951c-a9d988a2e21b" :question/math-academy-id "q-182530" :question/problem "If $A$ is false and $B$ is false, then $A⇔B$ is\n\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "According to the definition of the biconditional, \n\n- $A⇔B$ is true if $A⇒B$ **and** $B⇒A.$ Otherwise, it is false.\n\n- In other words, $A⇔B$ is true if $A$ and $B$ have the same truth values. Otherwise, it is false.\n\nSo, when $A=\\text{F}$ and $B=\\text{F},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A⇔B) & =(\\text{F}⇔\\text{F}) \\\\  & =\\text{T}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe complete truth table is shown below:\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,A⇔B\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n|  |  |  |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412689 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059777 :